1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:03,980
In this video, we're going to talk
about Linux system initialization.

2
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:08,135
So starting up the system and
getting our initial daemos and

3
00:00:08,135 --> 00:00:10,230
tasks started on the system.

4
00:00:10,230 --> 00:00:12,773
So we're going to talk about
Linux initialization and

5
00:00:12,773 --> 00:00:14,120
startup manager options.

6
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:16,346
It's the basics of writing
Linux in its scripts,

7
00:00:16,346 --> 00:00:18,157
which will also use in assignment five.

8
00:00:18,157 --> 00:00:23,542
So, there are a few different
mechanisms for initialization,

9
00:00:23,542 --> 00:00:28,341
Busybox is going to be the default for
buildroot projects.

10
00:00:28,341 --> 00:00:31,490
So that's the one that we're
going to use for assignment five.

11
00:00:31,490 --> 00:00:38,730
There's also system five, it's a system
V where V is a Roman numeral for five.

12
00:00:38,730 --> 00:00:42,960
System 5 in it, which is the default for
Yocto projects and

13
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:46,520
systemd is more sophisticated
startup manager.

14
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:49,524
That we're not going to really
cover in this course, but

15
00:00:49,524 --> 00:00:51,960
I just wanted to let you
know that it exists.

16
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:57,176
And of these three different options,
system five and busy box are both very

17
00:00:57,176 --> 00:01:02,440
similar, and systemd is kind of
a completely different in its system.

18
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:06,142
So for system 5 Init,
this was a 1980s era Unix system,

19
00:01:06,142 --> 00:01:09,350
it introduced and
included a concept of run levels.

20
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:14,322
And there were different run levels
happened based on whether you were in

21
00:01:14,322 --> 00:01:18,580
single multi user mode,
whether you had graphical support.

22
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:21,036
And then there was
a concept of startup and

23
00:01:21,036 --> 00:01:25,970
shutdown tasks that would happen when the
system was starting up or shutting down.

24
00:01:25,970 --> 00:01:30,362
So, in both system 5 and
Busybox cases, the startup and

25
00:01:30,362 --> 00:01:36,325
shutdown initialization related
functions are driven with shell scripts.

26
00:01:36,325 --> 00:01:39,298
And in the Busybox case specifically,

27
00:01:39,298 --> 00:01:44,440
this happens through a script
that's located at etc Init.d rcS.

28
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:49,395
So this is going to be invoked on startup
and it's going to have really simple logic

29
00:01:49,395 --> 00:01:54,371
that looks in the etc Init.d directory and
looks for files that start with S.

30
00:01:54,371 --> 00:01:58,495
And whatever those files are, it's
going to try to run them as a script and

31
00:01:58,495 --> 00:02:03,130
it's going to run them in the order that
specified based on the number after the S.

32
00:02:03,130 --> 00:02:08,062
So this really simple logic is
just going to first find all

33
00:02:08,062 --> 00:02:10,836
the different shell scripts and

34
00:02:10,836 --> 00:02:16,397
then it's going to execute here
the shell script that have found.

35
00:02:17,517 --> 00:02:24,816
So similar on shutdown,
the kill script is going to stop so,

36
00:02:31,580 --> 00:02:33,411
So in either the system 5 or
Busybox cases,

37
00:02:33,411 --> 00:02:35,794
the initialization is going to
be driven by shell scripts.

38
00:02:35,794 --> 00:02:37,132
In a Busybox case,

39
00:02:37,132 --> 00:02:42,170
etc Init.d rcS is going to be a really
simple script that looks like this.

40
00:02:42,170 --> 00:02:45,830
That's going to look for
any scripts that start with an S and

41
00:02:45,830 --> 00:02:49,940
list them based on the number
that's lifted after the S.

42
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:51,360
And based on the order,

43
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:55,489
is going to just call each script
with a start argument as shown here.

44
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:03,958
Similarly, on shutdown Busybox is
going to invoke etc Init.d rcK and

45
00:03:03,958 --> 00:03:05,960
that's just going to list.

46
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:11,200
Look through each of the etc Init.d start
scripts again, it's going to list them

47
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:15,982
in order and in this case it's going to
use the reverse order with Ls dash r.

48
00:03:15,982 --> 00:03:18,626
So in the opposite order
that they started,

49
00:03:18,626 --> 00:03:21,922
it's just going to call
the script with a stop argument.

50
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:31,442
So if your script that you place etc
Init.d just needs to start with an S.

51
00:03:31,442 --> 00:03:35,653
And a specific number giving the sequence
that you wanted to start up relative

52
00:03:35,653 --> 00:03:38,330
to the other scripts that
are in that directory.

53
00:03:38,330 --> 00:03:40,838
The script needs to support the start and

54
00:03:40,838 --> 00:03:45,730
stop parameters to work with the rc
scripts that I just mentioned previously.

55
00:03:45,730 --> 00:03:49,285
And the usual way to implement this
is to use it in a case statement,

56
00:03:49,285 --> 00:03:53,241
there is a good example of this in
mastering embedded links programming.

57
00:03:53,241 --> 00:03:57,668
Where you take the parameter that was
provided here as the first argument and

58
00:03:57,668 --> 00:04:00,240
you look to see whether it was start or
stop.

59
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:03,846
And then depending on which one it was,
you call start,

60
00:04:03,846 --> 00:04:07,560
stop daemon with either the dash S or
the dash K argument.

61
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:10,416
And you specify here the example
showing simple server,

62
00:04:10,416 --> 00:04:13,822
this is where you specify what your
application is going to be called.

63
00:04:13,822 --> 00:04:19,191
And this is where you specify where it
can be found in the root file system.

64
00:04:19,191 --> 00:04:23,230
So all the Init scripts
should you start stop daemon,

65
00:04:23,230 --> 00:04:26,115
except these arguments Dash S dash K.

66
00:04:26,115 --> 00:04:31,640
To set up whether you're starting or
killing the daemon and

67
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:36,400
with the dash K argument
it's going to send sig term.

68
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:40,859
So that was the signal that we mentioned
earlier that will allow graceful

69
00:04:40,859 --> 00:04:42,560
termination of a process.

70
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:46,255
So this gives your daemon a chance to
do any kind of clean up it needs and

71
00:04:46,255 --> 00:04:47,550
then gracefully exit.

72
00:04:47,550 --> 00:04:52,014
And we've already talked in a week four
about how you can write the daemon.

73
00:04:52,014 --> 00:04:57,050
So you can use that to write
your daemon that you'd reference

74
00:04:57,050 --> 00:05:01,601
here in the dash S version of start,
stopping daemon.

75
00:05:01,601 --> 00:05:05,177
You probably also for test purposes,
rather than needing to boot up the system,

76
00:05:05,177 --> 00:05:07,880
you probably want to test starting and
stopping your script.

77
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:11,692
So that's just as easy
as doing etc Init.d and

78
00:05:11,692 --> 00:05:16,101
then S whatever your daemon
script is named in start.

79
00:05:16,101 --> 00:05:19,988
Or etc Init.d S whatever your
system daemon is called stop and

80
00:05:19,988 --> 00:05:24,111
that will test that you're starting and
stopping it correctly.

81
00:05:24,111 --> 00:05:27,143
So you can use the PS command for instance
to make sure that it's either running or

82
00:05:27,143 --> 00:05:28,668
that it's been stopped successfully.